//! lotus/stereo_decimate.rye — ALES137: the sample-rate decimator carried into stereo, ONE SHARED RUN GRID, the time corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class. ALES83 named `decimate` — a zero-order hold: partition
//! a span into runs of `hold` samples anchored at `start`, and pin every sample in a run to the run's FIRST sample (its anchor), so a stream that entered at the full rate leaves at half, a quarter, a
//! sixteenth: the aliased, stepped, sample-and-hold voice of a converter read too slow. Where ALES136's bit-crush drops the low bits (the resolution axis) and the four amplitude corners shape the value, the
//! decimator shapes the THIRD axis — TIME — holding one sample across a whole run. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the
//! SAME length. This rung carries the decimator into stereo the plain way: run ALES83's proven mono `decimate` on each channel with the SAME hold, so one run grid lands on BOTH speakers together.
//!
//! The decimator is the HONEST REVERSE of the two not-odd members (ALES135 tube, ALES136 crush). Crush and tube compute NEW values, so they broke the inter-channel image; the decimator computes NO new number
//! at all — it COPIES an anchor across its run. Both channels share the same run grid (the same start and hold), so at each time index each channel holds ITS OWN anchor, and the stereo image at that index
//! equals the image that was at the run's anchor. The inter-channel relationship is therefore PRESERVED from the anchor — an out-of-phase master STAYS out of phase (right = −left holds through, since
//! right[i] = right[anchor] = −left[anchor] = −left[i]), a 1:2 master keeps its ratio at each held index, an identical-channel master stays identical — smeared in time, never split across channels. That is the
//! honest contrast the not-odd members lack, stated positively.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: the mono decimator carries NO pre-gain and NO grid — a pure time map, the crush's twin. Refuse BadHold (a hold outside [1, max_clip]) and BadRange (a span outside the samples) BEFORE
//! either channel is mutated — each fault depends only on the shared hold or the shared length, so each check is made ONCE up front, and a refusal never decimates one channel and leaves the other at the full
//! rate; otherwise decimate both channels over [start, start+count) by `hold` via ALES83's `decimate`. DecimateError reused whole — the stereo lift adds no fault.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO DECIMATE LAW: each channel equals ALES83's mono `decimate` with the same `hold` over the same span byte for byte — every sample equals its run's anchor, the runs
//! partitioned identically in each channel because the grid is shared. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length (a decimation copies values only). THE
//! SHARED-GRID IMAGE LAW: because both channels hold their own anchor at the same time index, the stereo image at each index equals the image at that run's anchor — an out-of-phase master STAYS out of phase, a
//! 1:2 master keeps its ratio at each held index, an identical-channel master stays identical (the honest reverse of the not-odd members, which broke the image); the map is idempotent, hold = 1 the identity.
//! THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal (BadHold, BadRange) leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; hold = 1 the identity on both, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES137.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-035333_fill-ales137-lotus-stereo-decimate.md
//! Stands on: lotus/decimate.rye (ALES83) — the mono zero-order hold run once per channel; lotus/stereo_crush.rye (ALES136) — the resolution-axis twin whose image-breaking this rung reverses; lotus/pan.rye
//! (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max, max_clip.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only through ALES83's own decimate, copying existing
//! samples and fabricating none, never a length; the `hold` is a sample count (unity = 1), the shape a zero-order hold, instantaneous per run (no interpolation, no anti-aliasing filter). No real sample rate, no
//! network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_decimate.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const decimate = @import("decimate.rye");

pub const stereo_decimate_version = "20260815.035333";

/// Stereo decimate's faults are exactly ALES83's own `DecimateError` — `BadHold` (a hold outside [1, max_clip]) and `BadRange` (a span outside the samples) — reused by name, since the stereo lift adds no new
/// fault: it only runs the same decimation on each channel after one shared validation.
pub const StereoDecimateError = decimate.DecimateError;

/// Decimate `[start, start+count)` in BOTH channels of `sc` by the same `hold`, the SAME run grid on each, running ALES83's proven mono `decimate`. Each run of `hold` samples is held to its first sample (its
/// anchor) — a zero-order hold on the time axis. `hold = 1` is the identity. Validates both channels once against the shared hold and length before either is mutated: refuses `BadHold` / `BadRange`, both
/// channels untouched. Values only — never the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_decimate(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32, hold: u32) StereoDecimateError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the hold and span are legal — refused by name before any write, so a refusal never decimates one channel and leaves the other at the full rate. The hold check depends only on the shared hold;
    // the span check on the shared length. These mirror ALES83's own guards exactly, so each mono decimation below is pre-validated to succeed.
    if (hold < 1) return error.BadHold;
    if (hold > decimate.max_hold) return error.BadHold;
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono decimation is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME run grid on each.
    try decimate.decimate(&sc.left, start, count, hold);
    try decimate.decimate(&sc.right, start, count, hold);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — a decimation copies values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO DECIMATE LAW — each channel equals ALES83's mono decimate with the same hold byte for byte; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — left.len == right.len after, both holding their starting length;
//     THE SHARED-GRID IMAGE LAW — the stereo image at each index equals the image at its run's anchor, an OUT-OF-PHASE master STAYING out of phase (the honest reverse of the not-odd members), a 1:2 master
//     keeping its ratio at each held index, an identical-channel master staying identical, the map idempotent, hold = 1 the identity; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — each refusal leaves both channels untouched
//     and balanced, hold = 1 the identity, count = 0 the identity ----

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO DECIMATE LAW — each channel equals ALES83's mono decimate with the same hold, proven side by side over one span with different per-channel samples; a hold of 2 keeps every other sample and
    //    repeats it once, per channel, the runs partitioned identically because the grid is shared.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 1000, -2000 }, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, 2500, -3000 });
        try stereo_decimate(&sc, 0, 4, 2); // hold 2

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 1000, -2000 });
        try decimate.decimate(&ml, 0, 4, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, 2500, -3000 });
        try decimate.decimate(&mr, 0, 4, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // by hand: hold 2 holds each pair to its first — {300,300,1000,1000} / {100,100,2500,2500}.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 300, 300, 1000, 1000 })) return error.LeftHandWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, 100, 2500, 2500 })) return error.RightHandWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a decimation copies values only. Proven at an even and an odd length (the odd length forcing a partial
    //    last run).
    {
        var even = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&even, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 300, 400 }, &[_]i16{ 400, 300, 200, 100 });
        try stereo_decimate(&even, 0, 4, 2);
        if (even.left.len != even.right.len) return error.EvenUnbalanced;
        if (even.left.len != 4) return error.EvenLength;

        var odd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&odd, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ 300, 200, 100 });
        try stereo_decimate(&odd, 0, 3, 2); // last run partial (one sample)
        if (odd.left.len != odd.right.len) return error.OddUnbalanced;
        if (odd.left.len != 3) return error.OddLength;
        // hold 2 over 3: runs [0,1]->anchor 0, [2]->anchor 2 (partial). left {100,100,300}, right {300,300,100}.
        if (!eq(odd.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, 100, 300 })) return error.OddPartialLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(odd.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 300, 300, 100 })) return error.OddPartialRightWrong;
    }

    // 3a. THE SHARED-GRID IMAGE LAW, the ANTISYMMETRY HOLDS — an out-of-phase master (right the exact negation of left) decimated through the same run grid STAYS out of phase, because the decimator copies each
    //     channel's own anchor at the same time index: right[i] = right[anchor] = −left[anchor] = −left[i]. This is the HONEST REVERSE of ALES135/136 (the not-odd members that broke the antisymmetry) — the
    //     decimator computes no new value, so it preserves the image.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, 300, -100, 500 }, &[_]i16{ -100, -300, 100, -500 }); // right = −left, out of phase
        try stereo_decimate(&sc, 0, 4, 2); // hold 2
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, r[i]) != -@as(i32, l[i])) return error.AntisymmetryBroke; // the image is PRESERVED — the contrast with the not-odd members
        }
    }

    // 3b. THE SHARED-GRID IMAGE LAW, the RATIO HOLDS — a 1:2 master (right twice left) keeps its 1:2 ratio at each held index, because both channels hold their own anchor at the same time index. Where a not-odd
    //     map compressed the ratio, the decimator preserves it.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1000, 400, 700, 200 }, &[_]i16{ 2000, 800, 1400, 400 });
        try stereo_decimate(&sc, 0, 4, 2); // hold 2 — left {1000,1000,700,700}, right {2000,2000,1400,1400}
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, r[i]) != 2 * @as(i32, l[i])) return error.RatioBroke; // 1:2 held at every held index
        }
    }

    // 3c. idempotence, and an identical-channel master stays identical — a held run holds to the same anchor, so decimating twice equals once on both channels; and a shared grid on equal inputs gives equal
    //     outputs.
    {
        var once = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&once, &[_]i16{ 700, -1500, 4000, 90, 12 }, &[_]i16{ -4000, 900, -700, 33, -8 });
        try stereo_decimate(&once, 0, 5, 3);
        var twice = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&twice, &[_]i16{ 700, -1500, 4000, 90, 12 }, &[_]i16{ -4000, 900, -700, 33, -8 });
        try stereo_decimate(&twice, 0, 5, 3);
        try stereo_decimate(&twice, 0, 5, 3);
        if (!eq(once.left.samples(), twice.left.samples())) return error.LeftNotIdempotent;
        if (!eq(once.right.samples(), twice.right.samples())) return error.RightNotIdempotent;

        var same = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&same, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 }, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 });
        try stereo_decimate(&same, 0, 4, 2);
        if (!eq(same.left.samples(), same.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad hold — a hold of zero refuses BadHold, both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        if (stereo_decimate(&sc, 0, 3, 0)) |_| return error.BadHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongBadHold;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 })) return error.BadHoldLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.BadHoldRightMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadHoldUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad span — a span past the samples refuses BadRange, both channels untouched.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7 }, &[_]i16{ 7, 6, 5 });
        if (stereo_decimate(&sc, 1, 3, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7 })) return error.BadRangeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 6, 5 })) return error.BadRangeRightMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE DEGENERATE LAW — hold = 1 makes each run one sample held to itself, so it is the identity on both channels; and count = 0 is the identity on both.
    {
        var unit = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&unit, &[_]i16{ 401, -299, 703 }, &[_]i16{ -701, 201, -401 });
        try stereo_decimate(&unit, 0, 3, 1); // identity — each run one sample
        if (!eq(unit.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 401, -299, 703 })) return error.UnitHoldLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(unit.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -701, 201, -401 })) return error.UnitHoldRightChanged;

        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 });
        try stereo_decimate(&noop, 1, 0, 4); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-decimate: the SAMPLE-RATE DECIMATOR CARRIED INTO STEREO, ONE SHARED RUN GRID, the time corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class — decimate a span in both channels of a master by the " ++
        "same hold, running ALES83's proven mono decimate on each (a zero-order hold, each run held to its anchor), so one run grid lands on both speakers together. A Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) " ++
        "whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. The decimator shapes TIME where the crush shapes resolution and the four DRIVE corners shape amplitude, and it is the HONEST " ++
        "REVERSE of the two not-odd members: crush and tube compute new values and broke the inter-channel image, but the decimator copies an anchor across its run and computes no new number, so because both " ++
        "channels share the run grid and each holds its own anchor at the same time index, the stereo image is PRESERVED from the anchor — an out-of-phase master STAYS out of phase and a 1:2 master keeps its " ++
        "ratio at each held index, smeared in time never split across channels. What the shared map keeps, per channel, is that every sample equals its run's anchor (a copy, never a new number), the map is " ++
        "idempotent (twice equals once), an identical-channel master stays identical, and hold = 1 is the identity. It validates both channels once against the shared hold and length before either is mutated " ++
        "(BadHold outside [1, max_clip], BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never decimates one channel and leaves the other at the full rate; DecimateError reused whole. The crux is four " ++
        "laws. THE STEREO DECIMATE LAW: each channel equals ALES83's mono decimate with the same hold byte for byte. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after and both hold their starting length. " ++
        "THE SHARED-GRID IMAGE LAW: the image at each index equals the image at its run's anchor, the antisymmetry and ratio both HELD (the honest reverse of the not-odd members), an identical-channel master " ++
        "staying identical, the map idempotent, hold = 1 the identity. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, hold = 1 the identity, count = 0 the identity. " ++
        "Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips decimated through the proven mono decimate, the hold a sample count and the shape a zero-order hold; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real " ++
        "speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
